# -- Q7: Design Limitations ----------------------------------------------------
pdf.add_page()
pdf.section('Question 7: Design Limitations')

pdf.subsection('Limitation 1: 2-DOF Legs Cannot Handle Lateral Terrain Variation')
pdf.body(
    'Each leg has only 2 DOF, both rotating about the sagittal (Y) axis. There is no '
    'abduction/adduction (side-to-side) DOF. As a result:\n'
    '- The robot cannot adapt to uneven terrain in the lateral direction.\n'
    '- Side slopes or obstacles perpendicular to the walking direction cannot be navigated.\n'
    '- A 3-DOF leg (adding a lateral hip DOF) would be required for rough terrain locomotion.\n'
    'This is an intentional simplification for this project, acceptable on flat ground.'
)

pdf.subsection('Limitation 2: Trot Gait is Dynamically Unstable at Low Speeds')
pdf.body(
    'The trot gait maintains only 2 feet in contact at any time. This is dynamically stable '
    'only when the robot maintains sufficient forward speed. At low speed or when stationary:\n'
    '- The CoM projection lies on the support line (not inside a polygon).\n'
    '- Active balance control (not implemented here) would be required.\n'
    '- A walk gait (3-foot contact) is statically stable but slower and more complex to '
    'implement in practice. The simple open-loop gait assumed here has no feedback.'
)

pdf.subsection('Limitation 3 (Bonus): Rigid Body Assumption')
pdf.body(
    'The model assumes rigid links and ideal actuators. In practice:\n'
    '- Joint compliance and motor torque limits affect the real motion profile.\n'
    '- Foot-ground contact is modelled as a point, ignoring friction and compliance.\n'
    '- These simplifications are appropriate for first-order design but would require '
    'refinement in a real implementation.'
)


